th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/2b-Probability-Distributions/joint-probability-density-function.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2b-Probability-Distributions/joint-probability-density-function.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_density_function_3cf1658a_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $(X,Y)$ have joint density $f_{X,Y}$, and let $B\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^2$ be a region. Which formula computes $\\mathbb{P}((X,Y)\\in B)$ for every such pair and set?","options":["$f_{X,Y}(x,y)$ at one point $(x,y)$ of $B$","$\\iint_B f_{X,Y}(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$","$\\iint_B f_X(x)f_Y(y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$","$\\sum_{(x,y)\\in B}f_{X,Y}(x,y)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d5ddcc01a8425273050d6bc1","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_density_function_3cf1658a_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which condition must a joint density satisfy?","options":["$0\\le f_{X,Y}\\le1$, since density values are probabilities","$\\int f_{X,Y}(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=1$ for every fixed $y$","$f_{X,Y}(x,y)=f_X(x)f_Y(y)$ for all $x,y$","$f_{X,Y}\\ge0$ with total integral $1$ over the plane"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"014468405cd77e7dbffdba3e","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_density_function_3cf1658a_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a pair with a joint density, what is $\\mathbb{P}(X=Y)$?","options":["$0$","$\\tfrac{1}{2}$, by symmetry","$\\int f_{X,Y}(x,x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","It depends on the density"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ff6eb26e075aeb91e4549ff9","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_density_function_3cf1658a_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A joint density is continuous at $(x,y)$ and satisfies $f_{X,Y}(x,y)=3$. For small positive $h$ and $k$, which expression approximates $\\mathbb{P}((X,Y)\\in[x,x+h]\\times[y,y+k])$?","options":["$3hk$","$hk$","$3(h+k)$","$9hk$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"81eb4eef47540b92f10444b3","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_density_function_3cf1658a_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which joint density describes independent $X$ and $Y$?","options":["$8xy$ on $0<x<y<1$","$x+y$ for $0<x,y<1$","$4xy$ on $(0,1)^2$","$2$ on $0<x<y<1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"977418fec2e4cdd12b2779c6","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_density_function_3cf1658a_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A point $(X,Y)$ is chosen uniformly in the unit square. Find the probability that it lies below the parabola, $\\mathbb{P}(Y<X^2)$.","options":["$\\tfrac{1}{2}$","$\\tfrac{1}{3}$","$\\tfrac{2}{3}$","$\\tfrac{1}{4}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"92713021b77dafcaaf99523f","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_density_function_3cf1658a_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For which constant $c$ is $f(x,y)=c\\,xy^2$ on the unit square a joint density?","options":["$3$","$2$","$\\tfrac{1}{6}$","$6$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7133e8dc927aeb1e85aa8655","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_density_function_3cf1658a_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"The pair $(X,Y)$ has density $3x$ on $0<y<x<1$ and zero elsewhere. Compute $\\mathbb{P}(Y>\\tfrac{1}{2})$.","options":["$\\tfrac{1}{2}$","$\\tfrac{1}{8}$","$\\tfrac{5}{16}$","$\\tfrac{3}{4}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"66e7dd8a1a3491dc123dc73d","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_density_function_3cf1658a_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A joint density is changed to the value $100$ on the segment $\\{(x,\\tfrac{1}{2})\\colon 0<x<1\\}$ and left unchanged elsewhere. What happens to probabilities?","options":["Every region meeting the segment gains probability","The total probability exceeds $1$","Nothing changes","Only probabilities of regions containing the whole segment change"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e229c9437e3f592fdf00e77c","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_density_function_3cf1658a_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The pair $(X,Y)$ has joint density $f$ satisfying $f(x,y)=f(y,x)$ for all $x,y$. Which statement about $\\mathbb{P}(X<Y)$ follows?","options":["It depends on how much density lies near the diagonal","It equals $\\tfrac{1}{2}$: equal mass on each side, none on the diagonal","It equals $\\tfrac{1}{2}$ only if $X$ and $Y$ are independent","It is below $\\tfrac{1}{2}$, since the diagonal carries some probability"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cffb937ae19eed3af017e4ae","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_density_function_3cf1658a_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A joint density $f$ is supported on $0<x<y<1$. Which iterated integral gives $\\mathbb{P}(Y<\\tfrac{1}{2})$?","options":["$\\int_{1/2}^1\\int_0^yf(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$","$\\int_0^{1/2}\\int_y^1f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$","$\\int_0^1\\int_0^{1/2}f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$","$\\int_0^{1/2}\\int_0^yf(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3c43643122b0eb58f122a1d5","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_density_function_3cf1658a_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"The pair $(X,Y)$ has joint density $f(x,y)=x+y$ on $(0,1)^2$ and zero elsewhere. What is $\\mathbb{P}(Y>2X)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{5}{24}$","$\\tfrac{1}{4}$","$\\tfrac{19}{24}$","$\\tfrac{1}{24}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"34f25deb35557815f612b53a","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_density_function_3cf1658a_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"$X\\sim\\operatorname{Exp}(2)$ and $Y\\sim\\operatorname{Exp}(1)$ are independent, with rates $2$ and $1$. Integrating the joint density, what is $\\mathbb{P}(X>2Y)$?","options":["$\\tfrac{1}{3}$","$\\tfrac{4}{5}$","$\\tfrac{1}{5}$","$\\tfrac{1}{2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d845bbabd584c01f93cdac82","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_density_function_3cf1658a_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Each proposed function is zero outside $(0,1)^2$. Which is a valid joint density?","options":["$\\dfrac{1}{4\\sqrt{xy}}$","$\\dfrac{1}{6x^{2/3}y^{1/3}}$","$\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{xy}}$","$\\dfrac{1}{3(xy)^{2/3}}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d539b3b78dffda21493883a5","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_density_function_3cf1658a_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $U,V$ be independent uniform variables on $(-3,3)$, and independently let $C\\in\\{0,1\\}$ satisfy $\\mathbb{P}(C=1)=\\tfrac{2}{5}$. Set $X=U$, with $Y=V$ if $C=0$ and $Y=U^2$ if $C=1$. Does $(X,Y)$ have a joint density?","options":["Yes, because both marginal distributions are continuous","Yes, because most probability is spread over a two-dimensional region","No, because dependence always rules out a joint density","No, because the parabola $y=x^2$ has probability $\\tfrac{2}{5}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"08bf549672c364d67f23dcec","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_density_function_3cf1658a_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"The pair $(X,Y)$ has density $4xy$ on $(0,1)^2$ and zero elsewhere. You are given $\\mathbb{P}(Y\\ge X+\\tfrac{1}{2})=\\tfrac{7}{96}$. Using the density's symmetry, find $\\mathbb{P}(|X-Y|<\\tfrac{1}{2})$.","options":["$\\tfrac{3}{4}$","$\\tfrac{7}{48}$","$\\tfrac{41}{48}$","$\\tfrac{89}{96}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a22b2615368c9a2cac483a3c","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_probability_density_function_3cf1658a_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"On the triangle $x>0$, $y>0$, $x+y<1$ a pair has density $6x$, and zero outside it. Find the probability that $X+Y<\\tfrac{2}{3}$ and $X>\\tfrac{1}{3}$ hold together.","options":["$\\tfrac{4}{27}$","$\\tfrac{8}{27}$","$\\tfrac{1}{9}$","$\\tfrac{23}{27}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e4021c78e6c9056f861c6bed","time_limit":40}]}}